Bank 5 — Model gateway — 60 Q&A
Focus: How to implement intelligent model routing, model abstraction, token optimization, cost governance, provider failover, security and evaluation-driven deployment.
Target roles: Principal Engineer – AI, AI Platform Architect and Director of AI Engineering.
1. Model Gateway Architecture
Q1. How would you implement a centralized Model Gateway?
Answer: Build a unified inference service using FastAPI and LiteLLM with centralized routing, authentication, budgeting and observability.
Q2. How would you abstract multiple model providers?
Answer: Implement provider adapters exposing a common inference interface for Bedrock, OpenAI, Azure OpenAI and self-hosted models.
Q3. How would you standardize model input and output?
Answer: Define canonical request and response contracts using Pydantic and translate provider-specific formats through adapters.
Q4. How would you implement model provider independence?
Answer: Use the adapter and strategy design patterns to decouple agent implementations from provider-specific APIs.
Q5. How would you make the Model Gateway horizontally scalable?
Answer: Deploy stateless gateway replicas behind a load balancer with distributed quotas and configuration storage.
Q6. How would you implement configuration-driven model routing?
Answer: Store routing rules, approved models, fallback chains and budget constraints in versioned YAML configurations.
Q7. How would you prevent agents from directly accessing model providers?
Answer: Restrict network egress and provider credentials so all inference requests must pass through the authorized Model Gateway.
Q8. How would you separate the Model Gateway control plane and execution plane?
Answer: Manage model registrations and routing policies in the control plane while enforcing inference decisions in the execution plane.
